Feature: The page that explains the encryption is the page that was approved

  A woman who is leaving another tracker does not want a promise. She wants to see what happens to
  one day of her life after she writes it down. This is the page that answers that, and it is the
  page a reviewer at the store opens when the listing claims the records are encrypted.

  Everything on it is a claim about safety, and a claim about safety is read in the way it is laid
  out as much as in the words. The three steps are numbered because the order is the point. The
  band that says who holds the keys is dark because it is the answer she came for. The four limits
  are given the same card as everything else, at the same size, because a limit drawn smaller than
  a benefit is a limit that was hidden.

  That page was drawn and approved on a canvas, and the drawing is committed beside this suite. The
  page the site serves is built from it by hand, in another language, through another framework.
  Nothing about that process keeps the two the same. A heading can fall back to another face and
  measure wider, a card row can reflow one column early, a tint can land a shade off, and every
  word is still there, so every test that reads words still passes.

  So this scenario does not read the page. It looks at it, beside the design, in one browser, and
  it answers one question: is she reading the page the operator approved. It runs at the width of a
  desktop, of a tablet, and of the phone most of the reading happens on, and on a failure it writes
  the picture of the difference, so the answer names the place rather than only the amount.

  The two bands the whole site shares are left out. Each drew presses to pages nobody has built yet,
  so the site holds those presses back and the board still draws them. What the page itself says is
  read whole, which is every claim this scenario exists for.

  Scenario: the privacy page matches the approved design pixel for pixel
    Given a woman opens the privacy page beside the design the operator approved
    Then what the page says about the encryption is as tall as the design, so no section is missing and none was added
    And what she reads about the encryption is the design, to within one pixel in a thousand
